Chapter: VirtioBlk

 The virtio-mmio transport, register layout and queue formats below are
 the device's, not ours: they are fixed by the VIRTIO specification and a
 reader cannot derive them from this code. Offsets, magic numbers, status
 bits and descriptor field order are quoted from the spec and are the only
 thing prose is carrying here.

 Slot geometry is a MACHINE property and is therefore a parameter, not a
 constant: QEMU virt puts virtio-mmio at 0x0a000000 with a 0x200 stride on
 arm64, and at 0x10001000 with a 0x1000 stride on riscv64. Probe the
 slots; do not assume one.

Section: Register offsets

  vb-off-magic : Integer = #000
  vb-off-version : Integer = #004
  vb-off-device-id : Integer = #008
  vb-off-device-features : Integer = #010
  vb-off-device-features-sel : Integer = #014
  vb-off-driver-features : Integer = #020
  vb-off-driver-features-sel : Integer = #024
  vb-off-queue-sel : Integer = #030
  vb-off-queue-num-max : Integer = #034
  vb-off-queue-num : Integer = #038
  vb-off-queue-ready : Integer = #044
  vb-off-queue-notify : Integer = #050
  vb-off-interrupt-status : Integer = #060
  vb-off-interrupt-ack : Integer = #064
  vb-off-status : Integer = #070
  vb-off-queue-desc-lo : Integer = #080
  vb-off-queue-desc-hi : Integer = #084
  vb-off-queue-driver-lo : Integer = #090
  vb-off-queue-driver-hi : Integer = #094
  vb-off-queue-device-lo : Integer = #0A0
  vb-off-queue-device-hi : Integer = #0A4
  vb-off-config : Integer = #100

 MagicValue spells "virt" little-endian. DeviceID 2 is a block device;
 DeviceID 0 means the slot is present but empty, which is how QEMU pads
 the array, so an empty slot is not the end of the scan.

  vb-magic : Integer = #74726976
  vb-device-id-block : Integer = 2
  vb-version-modern : Integer = 2

 Status bits, written cumulatively in this order by the spec's handshake.

  vb-status-acknowledge : Integer = 1
  vb-status-driver : Integer = 2
  vb-status-driver-ok : Integer = 4
  vb-status-features-ok : Integer = 8

 Request header: type (4 bytes), reserved (4), sector (8). Type 0 is a
 read, 1 a write. The status byte the device writes back is 0 for OK.

  vb-req-read : Integer = 0
  vb-req-write : Integer = 1
  vb-req-status-ok : Integer = 0

 Descriptor flags: NEXT chains, WRITE marks a buffer the DEVICE writes.

  vb-desc-next : Integer = 1
  vb-desc-write : Integer = 2

  vb-sector-size : Integer = 512

Section: Ring layout

 One queue, and the layout is ours to choose subject to the spec's
 alignment: the descriptor table must be 16-byte aligned, the available
 ring 2-byte, and the used ring 4-byte. Putting the used ring on a 4096
 boundary satisfies every version including legacy, at the cost of one
 page.

 Offsets are from the caller's ring base, which must itself be page
 aligned and must not overlap code or heap. Where that is belongs to the
 caller and not to this chapter: the plug knows its own memory map and
 passes the base in.

 An earlier draft of this paragraph named 0x40204000 and said a ring base
 belongs ABOVE the heap with the heap bumped past it. Both halves went
 stale within the day -- the ARM64 heap is at 0x40202000, and the rings
 ended up at 0x40010000, below the heap AND below the ELF, in the gap
 under the load address. A memory map named in the wrong chapter is a fact
 with no owner, so this one names none.

  vb-queue-size : Integer = 8

  vb-ring-desc : Integer = 0
  vb-ring-avail : Integer = 256
  vb-ring-used : Integer = 4096
  vb-ring-header : Integer = 8192
  vb-ring-status : Integer = 8208

  vb-ring-bytes : Integer = 12288

Section: Register access

  vb-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-read (base) (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (base + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  vb-write : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-write (base) (off) (v) = poke-32 (base + off) 0 v

Section: Probing

 A slot is this device when the magic matches, the version is the modern
 one, and the device id says block. Version 1 is the legacy transport
 with a different queue-setup path (QueuePFN rather than the split
 descriptor/driver/device addresses); it is REFUSED here rather than
 half-supported, because a legacy device configured through the modern
 registers accepts every write and then never completes a request.

  vb-slot-is-block : Integer -> Boolean
  vb-slot-is-block (base) =
   if vb-read base vb-off-magic /= vb-magic then False
   else if vb-read base vb-off-version /= vb-version-modern then False
   else vb-read base vb-off-device-id == vb-device-id-block

  vb-find-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-find-loop (base) (stride) (count) (i) =
   if i >= count then 0
   else if vb-slot-is-block (base + i * stride) then base + i * stride
   else vb-find-loop base stride count (i + 1)

 Answers the base address of the first block device, or 0 when the scan
 finds none.

  vb-find : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-find (base) (stride) (count) = vb-find-loop base stride count 0

Section: Initialisation

 The spec's handshake, in its required order: reset by writing 0, then
 ACKNOWLEDGE, DRIVER, feature negotiation, FEATURES_OK, queue setup, and
 DRIVER_OK last. Reading FEATURES_OK back is not decoration: the device
 clears it to refuse the feature set, and a driver that does not re-read
 it proceeds against a device that has already declined.

 No feature bits are requested. The block device's mandatory behaviour is
 all this driver uses, and every optional feature it might offer changes
 the request layout.

  vb-init-status : Integer -> Integer
  vb-init-status (base) =
   let r0 = vb-write base vb-off-status 0
   in let r1 = vb-write base vb-off-status vb-status-acknowledge
   in let r2 = vb-write base vb-off-status (vb-status-acknowledge + vb-status-driver)
   in let f0 = vb-write base vb-off-device-features-sel 0
   in let f1 = vb-write base vb-off-driver-features-sel 0
   in let f2 = vb-write base vb-off-driver-features 0
   in let r3 = vb-write base vb-off-status (vb-status-acknowledge + vb-status-driver + vb-status-features-ok)
   in bit-and (vb-read base vb-off-status) vb-status-features-ok

 The queue is set to exactly `vb-queue-size` or the device is refused,
 rather than accepting a smaller `QueueNumMax`. The ring slot is masked
 with `vb-queue-size - 1`, which is only a correct modulo when the ring is
 that size; taking a shorter queue would index past the available ring on
 the ninth request and corrupt whatever follows it. A device offering
 fewer than eight descriptors is refused loudly instead.

  vb-setup-queue : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-setup-queue (base) (ring) =
   let q0 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-sel 0
   in let maxq = vb-read base vb-off-queue-num-max
   in if maxq < vb-queue-size then 0
   else let size = vb-queue-size
   in let q1 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-num size
   in let d0 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-desc-lo (bit-and (ring + vb-ring-desc) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let d1 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-desc-hi (bit-shru (ring + vb-ring-desc) 32)
   in let a0 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-driver-lo (bit-and (ring + vb-ring-avail) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let a1 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-driver-hi (bit-shru (ring + vb-ring-avail) 32)
   in let u0 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-device-lo (bit-and (ring + vb-ring-used) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let u1 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-device-hi (bit-shru (ring + vb-ring-used) 32)
   in let q2 = vb-write base vb-off-queue-ready 1
   in size

 Answers the negotiated queue size, or 0 if the device refused the
 features or offers no queue 0.

  vb-init : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-init (base) (ring) =
   if vb-init-status base == 0 then 0
   else let size = vb-setup-queue base ring
   in if size == 0 then 0
   else let ok = vb-write base vb-off-status (vb-status-acknowledge + vb-status-driver + vb-status-features-ok + vb-status-driver-ok)
   in size

Section: Capacity

 Config offset 0 is the capacity in 512-byte sectors, 64 bits little
 endian. The high word is read but only the low 32 bits are returned:
 a disk beyond 2 TB is not addressable by the rest of this path anyway,
 and silently truncating a real value is worse than refusing one.

  vb-capacity : Integer -> Integer
  vb-capacity (base) =
   let hi = vb-read base (vb-off-config + 4)
   in if hi /= 0 then 0 - 1
   else vb-read base vb-off-config

Section: One-call entry points

 A plug's native builtin is a leaf routine with nowhere to hold state
 between calls, so these take the machine's slot geometry as arguments and
 do their own probing. That is also what keeps the geometry out of this
 chapter: the caller knows which machine it is on, and this chapter never
 has to.

 Capacity needs no queue. It is the probe and two config reads, so it
 exercises the MMIO path alone and answers before any ring exists, which
 makes it the cheapest thing to point at a real device first.

 Answers -1 when no block device is present, which `vb-capacity` also uses
 for a capacity it cannot represent. Both mean "do not believe a size from
 me", and neither can be confused with the 0 a device with no sectors
 would report.

  vb-capacity-auto : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-capacity-auto (mmio) (stride) (count) =
   let dev = vb-find mmio stride count
   in if dev == 0 then 0 - 1
   else vb-capacity dev

 A read has to probe and initialise as well, and it cannot do that on
 every call: writing status 0 resets the device, so re-running the
 handshake per read would reset a device that is already serving. The
 answer is one cached word just past the rings, holding the device base
 once the handshake has completed. Zero means not yet, which is also what
 the memory reads as before anything writes it, so the cache needs no
 separate initialisation.

  vb-cache-offset : Integer = 12288

  vb-ready : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-ready (mmio) (stride) (count) (ring) =
   let cache = ring + vb-cache-offset
   in let cached = bit-and (peek-32 cache 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in if cached /= 0 then cached
   else let dev = vb-find mmio stride count
   in if dev == 0 then 0
   else if vb-init dev ring == 0 then 0
   else let w = poke-32 cache 0 dev
   in dev

 Answers 1 on success and 0 on any refusal. `dest` must have room for 512
 bytes and is written by the DEVICE, so it must not be a Codex value the
 collector may move; the plug hands it a freshly bumped heap address.

  vb-read-auto : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-read-auto (mmio) (stride) (count) (ring) (sector) (dest) =
   let dev = vb-ready mmio stride count ring
   in if dev == 0 then 0
   else vb-read-sector dev ring sector dest

  vb-write-auto : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-write-auto (mmio) (stride) (count) (ring) (sector) (src) =
   let dev = vb-ready mmio stride count ring
   in if dev == 0 then 0
   else vb-write-sector dev ring sector src

Section: One read request

 Three descriptors, which is the spec's shape for a block request: the
 16-byte header the driver writes, the data buffer the DEVICE writes, and
 a one-byte status the device writes. Chaining is explicit through NEXT.

  vb-put-desc : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-put-desc (d) (addr) (len) (flags) (next) =
   let a0 = poke-32 d 0 (bit-and addr #FFFFFFFF)
   in let a1 = poke-32 (d + 4) 0 (bit-shru addr 32)
   in let l = poke-32 (d + 8) 0 len
   in poke-32 (d + 12) 0 (bit-or (bit-and flags #FFFF) (bit-shl next 16))

  vb-fill-header : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-fill-header (h) (kind) (sector) =
   let t = poke-32 h 0 kind
   in let r = poke-32 (h + 4) 0 0
   in let s0 = poke-32 (h + 8) 0 (bit-and sector #FFFFFFFF)
   in poke-32 (h + 12) 0 (bit-shru sector 32)

 Both rings put a 2-byte flags field at offset 0 and a 2-byte index at
 offset 2, so ONE aligned 32-bit read covers the pair and the index is its
 high half. Reading a 32-bit word at ring+2 to get the index directly
 would be an unaligned access, which is what the shift avoids.

  vb-ring-idx : Integer -> Integer
  vb-ring-idx (r) = bit-and (bit-shru (bit-and (peek-32 r 0) #FFFFFFFF) 16) #FFFF

  vb-set-avail-idx : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-set-avail-idx (a) (idx) = poke-32 a 0 (bit-shl (bit-and idx #FFFF) 16)

 An available-ring ENTRY is 16 bits, and the ring of them starts 4 bytes
 into the structure, so every odd entry sits at an address that is 2 mod
 4. There is no `poke-16` builtin -- only `poke-byte`, `poke-32` and the
 mmio forms -- and a `poke-32` there is an unaligned store.

 On these lanes the MMU is off, so an unaligned store is not slow, it is
 an ALIGNMENT FAULT. Measured 2026-08-16 on QEMU virt: entry 0 is at +4
 and stores fine, entry 1 is at +6 and takes
 `ESR=0000000096000061 FAR=0000000040010106` (data abort, DFSC 0x21) --
 so the FIRST read of any program succeeded and the SECOND killed the
 guest. Two byte stores, little end first, and the ring index no longer
 decides whether the machine survives.

  vb-put-avail-entry : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-put-avail-entry (e) (desc) =
   let lo = poke-byte e 0 (bit-and desc #FF)
   in poke-byte (e + 1) 0 (bit-and (bit-shru desc 8) #FF)

 The used index is the completion signal. It is polled rather than waited
 on because these lanes run with interrupts masked; the cap is the same
 shape as every other polling loop in the tree, and a timeout answers 0
 rather than spinning forever.

 The wait is for the index to REACH a value, not to be non-zero. The ring
 index is free-running across requests and wraps at 16 bits, so "has it
 moved" is the only question that stays true on the second read and every
 one after it.

  vb-poll-fuel : Integer = 2000000

  vb-await-used : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-await-used (ring) (want) (i) =
   if i >= vb-poll-fuel then 0
   else if vb-ring-idx (ring + vb-ring-used) == bit-and want #FFFF then 1
   else vb-await-used ring want (i + 1)

 A read and a write differ in exactly two fields: the header's type, and
 whether the data descriptor is marked WRITE (which means the DEVICE
 writes it). Everything else -- the three-descriptor chain, the available
 ring entry, the index bump, the notify, the used-ring wait, the status
 check -- is identical, so it is written once. The ring dance is where the
 alignment fault lived, and a second copy of it is a second place for that
 class of bug to hide.

 Answers 1 on success and 0 on any refusal: a device that never completes,
 or one that completes with a non-zero status byte. The data buffer must
 have room for 512 bytes.

  vb-request : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-request (base) (ring) (kind) (sector) (buf) (data-flags) =
   let h = ring + vb-ring-header
   in let st = ring + vb-ring-status
   in let d0 = ring + vb-ring-desc
   in let hdr = vb-fill-header h kind sector
   in let sclear = poke-byte st 0 #FF
   in let e0 = vb-put-desc d0 h 16 vb-desc-next 1
   in let e1 = vb-put-desc (d0 + 16) buf vb-sector-size (bit-or vb-desc-next data-flags) 2
   in let e2 = vb-put-desc (d0 + 32) st 1 vb-desc-write 0
   in let a = ring + vb-ring-avail
   in let cur = vb-ring-idx a
   in let want = bit-and (vb-ring-idx (ring + vb-ring-used) + 1) #FFFF
   in let slot = vb-put-avail-entry (a + 4 + (bit-and cur (vb-queue-size - 1)) * 2) 0
   in let bump = vb-set-avail-idx a (cur + 1)
   in let n = vb-write base vb-off-queue-notify 0
   in if vb-await-used ring want 0 == 0 then 0
   else if bit-and (peek-byte st 0) #FF /= vb-req-status-ok then 0
   else 1

  vb-read-sector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-read-sector (base) (ring) (sector) (dest) =
   vb-request base ring vb-req-read sector dest vb-desc-write

  vb-write-sector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vb-write-sector (base) (ring) (sector) (src) =
   vb-request base ring vb-req-write sector src 0
